WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.968 --> 00:00:12.790
Good afternoon everybody.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:15.990
Today, we're going to to do a session
on B2B product with a panel.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.230
Jason Lemkin is going to moderate.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:19.370
Thank you very much.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:24.540
We have Solomon from Docker,
Tracy from Plangrid, and Harry from Lob.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.490
Thank you all very much for coming.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:31.320
&gt;&gt; So, this is a great session, everyone
seen all the prior sessions online, right?

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.166
Raise your hand if you haven't.

00:00:32.166 --> 00:00:33.801
All right, good, 100%.

00:00:33.801 --> 00:00:35.260
They're great.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.376
So I watched Stewart Butterfield and
others.

00:00:40.376 --> 00:00:44.785
And what I thought would be particularly
fun since we have three amazing founders

00:00:44.785 --> 00:00:49.555
here of B2B companies, is to particularly
hone in on the intersection of building

00:00:49.555 --> 00:00:53.345
great products and revenue because we
all want to be product visionaries.

00:00:53.345 --> 00:00:56.310
I certainly did myself.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.100
But if you can't sell it and
it's a B2B product, ultimately,

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.790
what's the point of all of it.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:05.330
So I want to dig into a couple of stories
both in the early days and a little bit

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:10.500
later to help folks like you who probably
just have inklings on how to potentially

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.820
monetize your dreams because the stories
are always different than we think.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.040
So let me give 10 seconds
on each of the panelists,

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.960
then let me have them dig in a little
bit more on their companies.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.710
But Solomon from Docker, I don't know
who here knows about Docker, but

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:29.360
I'm guessing most of the 300,000
folks online that'll read it.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.240
But Docker, he'll give you some
more context, founded in 2010,

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.380
but not as Docker,
what was it called back then?

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:38.080
&gt;&gt; dotCloud.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.920
&gt;&gt; dotCloud, which we'll get to a story,

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.050
raised almost 200 million,
and Docker is sort of

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:48.030
a foundational piece of infrastructure
that everyone builds up around today.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:51.580
So we'll get into that, but it is
seven years into this journey, right?

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:54.378
Into a likely 70 year journey or
14 year journey.

00:01:54.378 --> 00:02:00.230
Tracy probably gets probably in
the middle of this rock star group.

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:01.990
Plangrid was found a little bit later.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:02.490
2011?
&gt;&gt; 2011.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:03.130
&gt;&gt; Got it.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:03.980
Raised 60 million?

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:05.820
&gt;&gt; Something like that.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.300
&gt;&gt; Right.
So money isn't everything, but

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:08.126
it does help.

00:02:08.126 --> 00:02:14.000
And Plangrid you'll learn is super cool,
automates construction management and

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.800
kind of has shaken up
a shockingly large industry,

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.910
shockingly large parts of software and

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:23.320
you'll learn a little bit more about
those stories and I want to learn more.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:28.240
And then Harry runs Lob which turns mail

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.060
into an API which I think
is pretty interesting.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:36.585
Raised $10 million and so
he's got another $190 million to catch up.

00:02:36.585 --> 00:02:39.550
$50 million, but
off to a very strong start.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:40.702
Founded in 2013?

00:02:40.702 --> 00:02:42.474
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:02:42.474 --> 00:02:43.207
&gt;&gt; What's that?

00:02:43.207 --> 00:02:46.100
[INAUDIBLE] Yeah, all right, and
in you're four years in, right?

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:49.570
So it's just when it's getting both
good and hard, right, at the same time.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:50.830
I would imagine, right?

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:55.330
So I want to begin but those are the
intros, but give me a little bit more

00:02:55.330 --> 00:03:00.240
Intro on the company like number of
employees, and what's the latest challenge

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.540
you have today, or
any other quick tidbits on the company.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:05.050
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:07.850
So currently it's 350 employees at Docker.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:10.930
We actually started in 2008.

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:11.760
&gt;&gt; 2008?
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.840
We're bootstrapped in France and
then it wasn't working, so

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.740
we moved here got into YC and
that really, really, really helped.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:19.750
So anyway.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:20.660
&gt;&gt; Got it.
&gt;&gt; Nine years.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:21.740
&gt;&gt; Nine years.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.450
&gt;&gt; You just sort of refound the company or

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:27.382
do you count those earlier so you-
&gt;&gt; It depends who you ask but yeah.

00:03:27.382 --> 00:03:29.640
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It counts, the same company,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.830
same problem we're trying to solve.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:35.010
So 350 employees, lot's of growth,
lot's of excitement.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:38.920
The biggest challenge for us, the biggest
challenge last year was proving that we

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:44.800
could build a business, a viable hi-growth
business on top of this excitement for

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:47.190
stuff that is in large part free,
in open source.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:52.410
That was last year,
we proved that model and

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.840
this year we have
the challenge is to scale it.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.823
And to scale it faster than our
competitors who now figured out that

00:03:57.823 --> 00:03:58.788
the model is proven.

00:03:58.788 --> 00:04:02.659
So it's a big competitive battle and
a really lucrative space, so

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:04.670
that's the challenge this year.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:09.100
&gt;&gt; Good.
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:10.680
&gt;&gt; Hi, everyone, my name is Tracy Young.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.000
I'm one of the cofounders and

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.100
CEO of Plangrid.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:16.910
Plangrid builds, as you said, beautiful,
simple, effective software for

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:18.920
the construction industry.

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.500
We emerged from
Y Combinators Winners 2012 batch.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:27.140
And since then, we've grown from
five co-founders to 310 people,

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:31.835
which is not something to brag about,
it means our payroll is very high.

00:04:31.835 --> 00:04:35.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But we've helped build over 500,000

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:39.560
construction projects from the smallest
kitchen remodels to the largest projects

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:43.900
in the world and
we are available on all the platforms.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:47.240
Apple actually named us one of
the top 10 enterprise apps of 2016,

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.700
which is pretty awesome.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.300
Now we're also the largest digital
blueprint repository in the world,

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:56.910
we have over 50 million sheets of
blueprints in our cloud and growing.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:00.050
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:01.050
So I'm sitting here.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.328
We've got 40 employees,
hoping I wish I had 150.

00:05:04.328 --> 00:05:06.493
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But a little bit about Lob,

00:05:06.493 --> 00:05:07.800
I'm one of the founders.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.690
My name's Harry Zhang.

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:14.273
Started company in 2013, and what we
do is build tools and make it easy for

00:05:14.273 --> 00:05:16.823
developers to control the offline world.

00:05:16.823 --> 00:05:20.300
So we actually deal with a physical
component, an industry that many of you

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:23.270
think may be dying, but in fact,
it is very much the opposite.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:27.910
Doing letters, postcards and
other types of mails and really building

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:32.000
a layer of technology on top of that to
make it easy for companies to do that.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.020
So hopefully, many of you have
received a piece of mail from Lob,

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:39.490
especially in this area and
the Bay Area and we service companies.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:44.027
We're trying to make this switch into
selling to mid-market, and other startups,

00:05:44.027 --> 00:05:46.180
and to selling it to the Fortune 500.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:49.930
So a lot of our challenges today is
really how do we move into Enterprise and

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:52.940
take what used to be
a lone single tool and

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:56.450
translate it into a larger full
solution for larger companies.

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:59.360
&gt;&gt; Good, so hang onto the microphone
because there's just one other question I

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.170
want to ask the whole panel.

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:06.250
But I really like to talk about the
concept of a minimum sale-able product,

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:10.420
not a viable product, like what it
actually takes to make real money.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:13.067
So Harry, tell me what happened with Lob,

00:06:13.067 --> 00:06:17.565
do not count other companies from
your batch, or customers [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:17.565 --> 00:06:20.478
How did you get those first
[INAUDIBLE] customers and

00:06:20.478 --> 00:06:22.830
what made the product sale-able?

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:27.040
&gt;&gt; Sure, yeah, I think one of the common
things that's easy to get in the habit of

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:30.290
thinking that customers are going to come
to you because you have a great product,

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:31.560
have a great solution.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:35.140
That works maybe in the beginning for
smaller companies, but

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:37.440
as you go after your first real customer,

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:40.820
our first real customer was
Oscar Insurance over in New York.

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:44.630
So the way we got to
them was cold email and

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:47.040
we sent thousands and thousands of emails.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:53.140
I personally wrote every single one and
got a response from the CEO.

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:56.120
And I think one of the key
things is to really

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:58.852
have an understanding of why
are companies going to talk to you?

00:06:58.852 --> 00:07:02.650
What is the problem you might be solving
for them as supposed to like here

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:05.450
is what my product does and
these are the features that I have, right?

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:08.722
You want to be very pinpoint
about what the message is.

00:07:08.722 --> 00:07:12.178
People are actually surprisingly open
to respond your email as long as it's

00:07:12.178 --> 00:07:13.330
relevant.

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:16.508
So for us, we happened to hit
on the problem for Oscar,

00:07:16.508 --> 00:07:20.935
which is they had a mail vendor and
they knew there was this regulation called

00:07:20.935 --> 00:07:26.400
HIPPA which is essentially how you govern
and control private health information.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.360
And what we realized was that
this was our opportunity and

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:30.610
they're looking at new vendors.

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:34.300
They did not trust their existing
vendor had HIPAA and was secure.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:37.590
And we could point to a small
customer we had already done it for

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.870
in the past and
that's how we got into the conversation.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:45.710
Took us about two or three months to get
the deal done, but really what you need is

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:49.410
to identify a core problem that larger
companies haven't quite figured out and

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:52.960
even if your solution isn't
the best that's out there,

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.180
it needs to be to a point where
the customers are willing to buy.

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:57.220
So we started off with one use case.

00:07:57.220 --> 00:07:59.740
Very, very simple explanation of benefits.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:04.120
Today we send probably 50
different types of mail, checks,

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:09.050
postcards, practically everything, but
But the key is to pick one small use case

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:12.400
that they have a real problem around and
that you're going to go solve.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:15.740
So that's what we did with Vosker and
he notes, and it's funny that he stayed,

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:17.950
he's still one of our larger customers,
and

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:21.100
it all just came from like a three
sentence email to their CEO.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.600
&gt;&gt; Do you remember what
the headline of the email was?

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:25.560
&gt;&gt; It was like,
we can help you with your mail.

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:28.279
&gt;&gt; We can help you with your mail,
that worked?

00:08:28.279 --> 00:08:28.799
&gt;&gt; It did work.

00:08:28.799 --> 00:08:31.560
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We've now learned a lot,

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:33.542
that's probably what I do today.

00:08:33.542 --> 00:08:38.316
But at the time we knew that healthcare
sent a lot of mail, and we also did

00:08:38.316 --> 00:08:43.358
that you've got mail, which got a pretty
good chuckle from folks in a day.

00:08:43.358 --> 00:08:47.190
But really did, the core of the content
was in the first two lines which was like,

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:50.710
we know companies have problems
to secure audible mail,

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:53.170
we built a technology
solution around doing this.

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:55.090
Do you have 50 minutes to talk about it.

00:08:55.090 --> 00:08:57.260
That's pretty much what
the core content was,

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:58.840
but the key is to get
straight to the point.

00:08:58.840 --> 00:08:59.810
What are you going to solve?

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:03.020
What's the problem and asking for
a little bit more time.

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:07.270
I never talk to CEO but he pounded me down
the chain but that got right eventually.

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:12.000
&gt;&gt; So Tracy, I'm not an expert but
you're probably competing with

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.300
bigger traditional older incumbents
in some ways than Lob was, right?

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:16.360
Do I have that wrong?

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:19.590
What got you salable,
what got you really off the ground?

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:22.080
Beautiful software is great, but
we all want to build that, right?

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:23.510
What made it salable?

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:28.420
&gt;&gt; Sure, our first ten users,
I'm trained as a construction engineer, so

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:32.260
the first ten users were definitely
people we had worked with in the field.

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:34.830
And it's easy to get our friends
to try out our software.

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:37.540
It's incredibly difficult to get
our friends to start paying for it.

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:38.430
So-
&gt;&gt; That is a difference.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:41.800
&gt;&gt; Building something that they wanted and
that they needed that

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:46.140
added undeniable value to their day to day
was the moment they started paying us.

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:48.960
&gt;&gt; Was the feedback from your
non paying friends useful?

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:50.240
Was it unhelpful?

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:51.000
Was it dishelpful?

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:54.810
&gt;&gt; So our first beta group which included

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:57.180
mostly people I'd worked with in
the field was about 30 people.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:00.450
29 of them started paying us
as soon as the beta was over.

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:01.350
&gt;&gt; They did pay you.

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:02.220
&gt;&gt; Hm-mm.
&gt;&gt; Got it.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:04.719
So were they bigger critics
than outside customers?

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:09.024
Sometimes it's hard to figure on how
to take advantage of friends as your

00:10:09.024 --> 00:10:09.648
own customers.

00:10:09.648 --> 00:10:10.828
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
yeah you're really testing my memory.

00:10:10.828 --> 00:10:15.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So no, it was at that point in 2011,

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:19.870
2012 is about I want this to do more for
us.

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:21.620
Can you build the XYZ?

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:26.060
&gt;&gt; Got it, and obviously the product
was FuturePoint at the time, right?

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.180
What was the one piece
that was 10X better?

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:31.850
&gt;&gt; So blueprints are at the core.

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:34.385
PlanGrid replaces physical blueprints.

00:10:34.385 --> 00:10:36.400
Fold-out maps, we don't have them anymore.

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:37.230
Actually with this group,

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:39.364
you guys have probably never
purchased a fold-out map before.

00:10:39.364 --> 00:10:41.590
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But we use Waze, we use Apple Maps,

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:44.270
we use Google Maps, and
with blueprints, it's the same analogy.

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:49.100
These massive, incredibly dense
pieces of paper with information, and

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:52.590
they just don't render
quickly on PDF viewers.

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:56.050
So it was just-
&gt;&gt; So what was the one line,

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:58.290
what was the pitch to make your
beautiful product salable?

00:10:58.290 --> 00:10:59.005
Was it [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; I'm really glad.

00:10:59.005 --> 00:11:01.420
I was hoping you would ask
me what the tag line was.

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:02.030
&gt;&gt; What was the tag line?

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:05.140
&gt;&gt; Our tag line, and
this is from my co-founder, Kenny.

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:08.910
Our tag line was Plangrid
eliminates the ****.

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:10.550
Except that marketing has now changed it.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:14.759
So we're the productivity software for
contraction.

00:11:14.759 --> 00:11:15.640
&gt;&gt; Got it, okay.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:21.700
We'll dig more on that, so boy,
where do we want to answer this questions.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:24.722
So we start hosting applications,
right for others?

00:11:24.722 --> 00:11:26.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; What's their early history?

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:29.425
Tell us what didn't work, or at least
didn't scale and that what did work.

00:11:29.425 --> 00:11:31.030
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; So you can sell the product.

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:32.990
&gt;&gt; So the background here is, so

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:37.000
nine years in, we started out as
a company called dotCloud, and now

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:40.020
we're a different company called Docker,
but there is a common thread, right?

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:43.185
It's the same company, it's the same
team with the same mission but

00:11:43.185 --> 00:11:44.265
we changed how we do it.

00:11:44.265 --> 00:11:50.925
And the first phase of dotCloud, well, let
me start with what the common thread is,

00:11:50.925 --> 00:11:53.225
the common thread is we help
developers be more productive.

00:11:53.225 --> 00:11:56.010
We give them tools to create applications.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:59.760
And then we help the businesses
behind those developers

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:03.930
solve their business problems
with the creation of software.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:07.140
And we're serving on these
trends of more people

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:09.860
creating software to do more things.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:13.980
So at the first phase,
we did that by hosting the applications.

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:17.390
So we said, just write the code, upload it
at to us, we'll take care of everything,

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:19.404
we'll run it for you,
we'll manage it for you, all of it.

00:12:19.404 --> 00:12:27.320
And, and then now, as Docker, we don't
do the hosting, we provide the tools.

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:31.900
You use the tools yourself, and
then you host the application and

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:37.270
eventually as a business,
you need help with various aspect of

00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:40.900
distributing your software at large scale.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:47.520
So in the first phase, we're very small,
we didn't have any sort of hype behind us.

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:50.840
We were just trying to convince
developers to use our stuff.

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:55.330
Honestly we used our own community, where

00:12:57.150 --> 00:13:01.080
programmers, we write software, and
we know a lot of other programmers and

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:04.170
we just annoyed the hell out of
all of them to use our stuff.

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:10.920
We gave it to them for free, and then kind
of through the individual programmers,

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:14.220
eventually made our way to
the business behind the programmer.

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:19.960
So the difference I think is that,
for us it's a two step process

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:26.230
that our users and our biggest advocates
are not the people paying for the product.

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:30.330
So, that means we have to
give a lot of stuff away for

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:35.030
free before we even get the first
conversation with the actual buyer.

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:35.530
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:40.110
&gt;&gt; So those first early years,

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:43.040
it's not even the same product
you have today remotely, right?

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:43.760
How did you survive?

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:48.140
Did you raise 50 million
in your seed round?

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:50.450
&gt;&gt; No.
&gt;&gt; How did you pull this off, right?

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:52.580
Surviving's the hard part, right?

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:54.170
&gt;&gt; Well, so
when you;'re a very small team,

00:13:54.170 --> 00:14:00.000
you have kind of a limited window of time
where you could survive and earn a lot.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:00.570
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:03.150
It's hard usually for that to last
more than 24 months, isn't it?

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:03.650
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:14:03.650 --> 00:14:08.930
But, yeah, so
we joined Y Combinator in 2010 and

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:16.640
raised our first small round of funding
at the end of 2010, so within six months.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:18.710
And we just went at it gradually.

00:14:20.180 --> 00:14:23.520
I mean,
it's nothing particularly unique there.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:27.010
&gt;&gt; But you got into YC about
24 months after founding.

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:28.370
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
the frist part was really tough,

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:31.495
it was bootstraps,
we did consulting, we did whatever.

00:14:31.495 --> 00:14:32.670
&gt;&gt; Consulting was the hack.
&gt;&gt; It was the hack,

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:35.620
whatever we had to do to pay the bills,
it was really tough.

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:41.026
My advice would be consulting and
product building,

00:14:41.026 --> 00:14:45.290
they're very useful but
you gotta get out of it

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:48.230
really quickly because the consulting
takes all of your time.

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:51.580
You will never get off
the ground as a product company.

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:53.200
There's too much distraction
in consulting, so

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:55.240
eventually we just took a leap of faith.

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:56.460
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:58.890
&gt;&gt; And
landed on our feet in Y Combinator, so.

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:02.280
&gt;&gt; Got it, and then one last question and
then I want to move on to the next topic.

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:05.540
But so what is now Docker
has gone through two and

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:07.230
a half evolutions of business model,
right?

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:10.220
And you've made Open Source work for
you, right?

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:11.510
What's the secret there?

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:12.620
What's the learning?

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:15.040
because we think it's hard
to monetize Open Source.

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:17.680
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
honestly I don't know how useful

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:20.760
my insights are here because really
the trick is to get really lucky.

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:25.649
[LAUGH] And
the situation we're in now that

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:31.672
the Open Source technology that we
developed, Docker, is extremely popular.

00:15:31.672 --> 00:15:32.320
&gt;&gt; It is.

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:34.310
&gt;&gt; We were in the right
place at the right time,

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:39.640
there's this whole trend on containers
which we kind of help start.

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:44.110
And now, just the demand for
it is honestly hard to describe.

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:46.860
So, you

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:49.770
start having a completely different set of
problems once you are in that situation.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:53.340
So, honestly the problems we had
before that moment in 2013 when

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:57.590
we launched the Open Source Docker and
after, our world's apart.

00:15:57.590 --> 00:15:58.340
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Now,

00:15:58.340 --> 00:16:02.886
honestly it's more, How do we
deliver on the incredible demands

00:16:02.886 --> 00:16:06.309
fast enough before
the competitors do it instead?

00:16:06.309 --> 00:16:11.657
So there is no outbound in our case,
the question is too much inbound.

00:16:11.657 --> 00:16:13.881
Which seems like nice a thing,
but it's actually-

00:16:13.881 --> 00:16:15.016
&gt;&gt; It's noise.

00:16:15.016 --> 00:16:19.669
&gt;&gt; It's noise and
also you drop a lot of it on the floor and

00:16:19.669 --> 00:16:22.275
your competitors pick it up.

00:16:22.275 --> 00:16:27.751
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so, Tracy,
I want to come back to your fun story.

00:16:27.751 --> 00:16:32.872
You hit up 30 of your friends from
construction and 29 became customers.

00:16:32.872 --> 00:16:36.650
As you scale,
what do you do with customer feedback?

00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:37.680
How important is this?

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:38.610
How much do you listen to?

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:40.270
How much of it is noise?

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:42.500
How much do you let it guide your roadmap?

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:43.060
What have you learned?

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:47.265
&gt;&gt; I don't know how you build
product without listening to your

00:16:47.265 --> 00:16:48.160
customers' feedback.

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:51.780
We certainly take it incredibly seriously,
we've logged and

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:57.057
categorized every single piece of feedback
that our clients have given us since 2011.

00:16:57.057 --> 00:16:59.030
It's incredibly easy
we've made that process.

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:02.222
You just come on plangrid.com and
you're like, I want to give you feedback.

00:17:02.222 --> 00:17:05.690
And then our support team will take it and
they'll ship it over to the product team.

00:17:05.690 --> 00:17:08.430
We'll look at it every quarter and
it does help us prioritize.

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:14.016
The big task here is making sure that
we're prioritizing the right stuff.

00:17:14.016 --> 00:17:18.291
Because sometimes our users will ask us,
can PlanGrid make up my breakfast and

00:17:18.291 --> 00:17:18.825
dinner.

00:17:18.825 --> 00:17:22.409
And so,
when there's large general contractors and

00:17:22.409 --> 00:17:27.517
smaller subcontractors and building
owners asking us for the same button.

00:17:27.517 --> 00:17:31.802
And telling us that if you just have this
thing, it's going to save me so much time,

00:17:31.802 --> 00:17:34.870
and there's enough domain
experience on the team.

00:17:34.870 --> 00:17:37.050
When so many customers are asking for
the same thing,

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:41.640
it's probably the right thing to build,
although tracking is obviously important.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:46.290
But we typically look at it as,
how hard is this to build?

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:50.235
How much resources do we have
to put into this versus,

00:17:50.235 --> 00:17:54.100
how much value and
impact is it going to give our users?

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:57.990
And if it's low level of effort and high
impact, we're going to prioritize that.

00:17:57.990 --> 00:17:58.720
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:02.520
&gt;&gt; And that's really helped
us guide our product roadmap.

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:05.420
&gt;&gt; And have you had any of
your bigger customers drag

00:18:05.420 --> 00:18:07.870
your roadmap in a different way
than your smaller customers?

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:17.875
&gt;&gt; Yes, the larger customers, they want to
be treated like princes and princesses.

00:18:17.875 --> 00:18:23.220
That they're running our entire business,
but they're incredible supportive of us.

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:27.040
It's nice that not any
single one customer has

00:18:27.040 --> 00:18:28.764
that big of a percentage of our revenue.

00:18:28.764 --> 00:18:32.554
And so they just want to make sure
that we're listening to them, and

00:18:32.554 --> 00:18:36.213
that we're showing them what
the future of PlanGrid looks like,

00:18:36.213 --> 00:18:38.630
and that we're constantly improving it.

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:41.084
Because they're not only
buying PlanGrid today, but

00:18:41.084 --> 00:18:43.490
they're buying PlanGrid
six months from now.

00:18:43.490 --> 00:18:44.360
It's a committment, right?

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:44.900
It's a relationship.

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:46.460
&gt;&gt; It's a long term commitment, right?

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:50.068
And so, how many customers
total do you have at PlanGrid?

00:18:50.068 --> 00:18:50.796
&gt;&gt; Tens of thousands.

00:18:50.796 --> 00:18:51.428
&gt;&gt; Tens of thousands?

00:18:51.428 --> 00:18:52.776
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; But the revenue distribution,

00:18:52.776 --> 00:18:55.792
it's probably several orders of magnitude
at your biggest customers, right?

00:18:55.792 --> 00:18:57.777
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; When even if it's a couple percent of

00:18:57.777 --> 00:18:59.269
revenue or maybe in the early days,

00:18:59.269 --> 00:19:02.155
when they ask you to build something
that's kind of on your roadmap,

00:19:02.155 --> 00:19:05.400
that's way out there,
because this is an issue we all face.

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:07.350
Did you build it?

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:09.700
The B2C guys always say no, right?

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:13.780
But when your big customer wants you to
build a 2019 feature, do you build it?

00:19:13.780 --> 00:19:16.805
&gt;&gt; Again, if there's like,
a ton of the big customers asking us for

00:19:16.805 --> 00:19:19.190
the same thing,
it's easy to prioritize that.

00:19:19.190 --> 00:19:22.622
But, we have never built a feature out for

00:19:22.622 --> 00:19:24.670
just one customer,
that doesn't make sense.

00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:25.610
We're building software for

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:29.010
the construction industry,
not XYZ Construction.

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:29.630
&gt;&gt; Got it, yeah.

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:33.120
Harry, Lob, is there a difference
between different sized customers?

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:37.180
&gt;&gt; Definitely a big difference
between our large and small customers.

00:19:37.180 --> 00:19:40.320
We spend a lot of time focusing
on the larger customers.

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:44.010
I think one thing you mentioned earlier,
right?

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:46.380
Do our larger customers
impact our roadmap?

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:48.210
They certainly do, right?

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:51.775
That said, I think it's also about
being transparent in the conversation,

00:19:51.775 --> 00:19:53.840
from the first deal,
like what are you building?

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:56.090
You're not just selling
one particular feature,

00:19:56.090 --> 00:19:58.230
one particular item,
it's a roadmap, right?

00:19:58.230 --> 00:20:01.990
It's like you're investing at Lob, you're
going to be building infrastructure on it.

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:04.760
We need to talk to that roadmap and
when larger customers are purchasing,

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:07.170
they're looking at what are we
building six months from now,

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:09.150
trying to understand what that is.

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:11.850
And for us, it's about finding
the balance between the type of

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:12.570
customers that we want.

00:20:13.655 --> 00:20:17.035
And listening to those customers a little
bit more that are more strategic.

00:20:17.035 --> 00:20:20.055
So for us, we're selling something
that's very ubiquitous, right?

00:20:20.055 --> 00:20:22.564
Mail comes in many shapes,
formats, sizes, and

00:20:22.564 --> 00:20:26.046
there's literally endless amounts
of combinations that we can do.

00:20:26.046 --> 00:20:28.389
And if we entertain every single request,

00:20:28.389 --> 00:20:31.325
we'd end up going a million
directions all the time.

00:20:31.325 --> 00:20:36.140
So for us, we focus very
strategically on operational mail.

00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:38.280
So mail that customers are compliant and

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:41.630
required to send as
opposed to marketing mail.

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:44.270
That's not to say we don't do a lot
of marketing mail, we still do.

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:47.244
But when we think about
roadmap conversations and

00:20:47.244 --> 00:20:49.451
it comes to a trade off between A or B.

00:20:49.451 --> 00:20:51.570
We're always going to look
at the customer who one,

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:53.190
we have a relationship with, right?

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:56.540
Two is the direction that we want
the company to move to begin with, and

00:20:56.540 --> 00:20:57.620
that's how we prioritize it.

00:20:57.620 --> 00:20:59.324
And sometimes,
that means saying no to customers.

00:20:59.324 --> 00:21:01.292
And oftentimes we found
they're okay with that, so

00:21:01.292 --> 00:21:03.626
long as you're transparent
about what you're trying to do.

00:21:03.626 --> 00:21:05.374
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Solomon, let's talk,

00:21:05.374 --> 00:21:09.795
you said in the beginning you guys were
five co-founders for two years, right?

00:21:09.795 --> 00:21:10.501
I think.

00:21:10.501 --> 00:21:11.324
&gt;&gt; No, that.

00:21:11.324 --> 00:21:12.070
&gt;&gt; Roughly?

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:13.560
I have that wrong?

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:16.020
&gt;&gt; No, I think I heard five co-founders
&gt;&gt; Was it Tracy five?

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:17.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; We'll get into equity splits later.

00:21:17.930 --> 00:21:19.234
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; At minute 51,

00:21:19.234 --> 00:21:21.252
until I learn how to add, okay?

00:21:21.252 --> 00:21:27.359
But any related questions, so
I wanted to ask most of the panel this.

00:21:27.359 --> 00:21:30.510
As you scale, let's talk about,
because this is always a great learning,

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:31.360
a mishire, right?

00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:35.750
So as you get out your core team,
is there a core functional area or VP or

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:39.090
someone that you thought you needed or
got wrong in the early days?

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:40.380
Anything you can learn from?

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:42.610
What's the biggest hiring
mistake you've made?

00:21:42.610 --> 00:21:44.147
&gt;&gt; I don't know about the biggest but

00:21:44.147 --> 00:21:45.274
we made a lot-

00:21:45.274 --> 00:21:46.730
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:47.961
&gt;&gt; We made a lot,

00:21:47.961 --> 00:21:50.945
I don't know anyone who hasn't.

00:21:50.945 --> 00:21:53.350
&gt;&gt; Yes, but we can learn from this for
the next week, right?

00:21:53.350 --> 00:21:54.693
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we can.

00:21:54.693 --> 00:21:56.490
&gt;&gt; This mistake a fewer times.

00:21:56.490 --> 00:22:02.191
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, the biggest challenge is
once you've grown to full-fledged company,

00:22:02.191 --> 00:22:05.740
then you grow outside
your zone of comfort.

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:08.259
If you're in the technical and
product side,

00:22:08.259 --> 00:22:11.754
then you'd have to hire business
people for the business side.

00:22:11.754 --> 00:22:15.680
You have to hire more technical and
product leadership.

00:22:15.680 --> 00:22:18.640
And, it's things that at
some point you don't know.

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:20.667
And you don't know what you don't know.

00:22:20.667 --> 00:22:23.598
And I remember as an engineer,
hiring non-engineers,

00:22:23.598 --> 00:22:25.130
it was very difficult for me.

00:22:25.130 --> 00:22:30.000
Because I knew I needed help but
I didn't have the know-how to

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:35.350
distinguish the different kinds
of non-engineers that I needed.

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:39.862
It seems really silly to say that,
but to me marketing and

00:22:39.862 --> 00:22:43.810
sales, it was like the not coding thing.

00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:50.310
And also, I moved to the US from France,

00:22:50.310 --> 00:22:53.610
and Americans are extremely
good at selling themselves.

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:55.330
&gt;&gt; You know I hear a lot
from European founders.

00:22:55.330 --> 00:22:56.670
I hear that as a consistent theme.

00:22:56.670 --> 00:22:59.120
&gt;&gt; And so you're out here,
and you meet ten people.

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:03.734
Wow, these are all rock stars [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Hire them right then and there.

00:23:03.734 --> 00:23:05.094
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and you do.

00:23:05.094 --> 00:23:06.703
And then you realize,
okay, there's more to this.

00:23:06.703 --> 00:23:11.463
And the biggest problem is you're
when you hire the wrong person,

00:23:11.463 --> 00:23:15.250
the state of mind is, it's a bad person.

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:18.900
They're bad and my company's good and
now I hired the bad person.

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:23.340
But really what's happening is,
everyone's wasting their time.

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:25.990
It's not the right
combination of people and

00:23:25.990 --> 00:23:30.200
you're going to find out six months later
and it's going to be a terrible nuisance.

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:35.758
So I don't really know if I've
learned anything how to avoid it.

00:23:35.758 --> 00:23:40.642
But, every time we made a mistake, we've
tried to really invest the time to go

00:23:40.642 --> 00:23:43.240
back and find out why, what we did wrong.

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:46.670
So that we don't make the same
mistake twice, honestly.

00:23:46.670 --> 00:23:49.630
And I guess.

00:23:49.630 --> 00:23:52.736
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; It's not the hired's fault,

00:23:52.736 --> 00:23:54.718
it's your fault, isn't it?

00:23:54.718 --> 00:23:56.600
&gt;&gt; Yeah, exactly, that's exactly right.

00:23:56.600 --> 00:23:59.566
And my feeling is it's
the hardest at the beginning.

00:23:59.566 --> 00:24:02.026
But if you find,
once you have a core group of people,

00:24:02.026 --> 00:24:06.840
like five key people in areas that you
don't really Understand and master, but

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:08.960
you build a relationship
of trust with those people.

00:24:08.960 --> 00:24:10.380
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Then it gets easier because you

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:11.920
trust them to hire more people.

00:24:13.060 --> 00:24:16.190
The really bad situation is if you
don't sort out that stuff early on.

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:18.540
Like you make a hire,
you feel like it might be a bad hire,

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:20.820
but you don't deal with it right away.

00:24:20.820 --> 00:24:24.300
You allow it to Fester.

00:24:24.300 --> 00:24:26.400
And then before you know it,
you're growing and

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:28.370
that person has hired 20 more people.

00:24:28.370 --> 00:24:30.080
And now, you're in big trouble.

00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:33.390
Because now, you gotta deal with
the mismatch, potentially, with 20 people.

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:34.940
So my only advice would be,

00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:38.520
you're going to make mistakes, just deal
with them early and listen to you're gut.

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:42.650
If your gut says, maybe this is
the wrong thing, trust yourself,

00:24:42.650 --> 00:24:44.200
even if it it's not your
field of expertise.

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:48.810
I mean, I'm probably repeating
common sense here, but-

00:24:48.810 --> 00:24:50.438
&gt;&gt; We all make the same mistake.

00:24:50.438 --> 00:24:52.444
Tracy, I want to hear your story too,
but let me flip it around.

00:24:52.444 --> 00:24:54.448
You've five co-founders,right?

00:24:54.448 --> 00:24:58.571
So you've got, you felt like you had
at least two of the bases covered in

00:24:58.571 --> 00:25:01.670
the early days, at least in theory, right?

00:25:01.670 --> 00:25:05.950
What was one of these early hires you
made that was extremely impactful,

00:25:05.950 --> 00:25:08.370
that moved the needle,
that helped increase the business?

00:25:09.560 --> 00:25:10.870
&gt;&gt; Our employee number one.

00:25:10.870 --> 00:25:12.610
&gt;&gt; Employee number one, who was that?

00:25:12.610 --> 00:25:13.210
&gt;&gt; Cora Lorenz.

00:25:16.340 --> 00:25:19.800
Where early on there's nothing, and
you have to build something from nothing.

00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:23.180
The more people that we can
get on the team to help

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:26.540
realize our mission as a company
is incredibly important.

00:25:26.540 --> 00:25:28.200
And I would say the same
about employee number two and

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:30.090
employee number three and
employee number 300.

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:30.600
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:33.970
Was there a particular functional area or
someone that changed things for you?

00:25:35.940 --> 00:25:36.505
They're all great.

00:25:36.505 --> 00:25:39.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean we brought them on because we

00:25:39.130 --> 00:25:40.120
needed help, right?

00:25:40.120 --> 00:25:43.370
So it's hard to just pinpoint one person.

00:25:43.370 --> 00:25:45.186
&gt;&gt; For sure,
well let me ask you a different question.

00:25:45.186 --> 00:25:50.207
You have five founders, when do you
bring on VP's or Directors or others to

00:25:50.207 --> 00:25:55.722
take over the function layers, from that
what do you learn from that experience?

00:25:55.722 --> 00:26:00.646
&gt;&gt; The point where we realize that as
being a founder especially doing it for

00:26:00.646 --> 00:26:05.084
the first time, so much of our jobs
is just learning on the spot and

00:26:05.084 --> 00:26:06.959
trying to get the job done.

00:26:06.959 --> 00:26:10.581
As we've recruited more and more people,
the team, there comes a point where it's

00:26:10.581 --> 00:26:13.510
like, wow, we have to become
really good at managing people.

00:26:13.510 --> 00:26:15.810
And this is all new for the founders.

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:17.160
I think we got pretty good at it,

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:20.550
especially as just a frontline
engineering manager.

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:24.100
But there comes to a point
where we're at 50 people,

00:26:24.100 --> 00:26:28.130
150 people, and it's like Dunbar's
numbers, like complete chaos.

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:30.970
Suddenly communication gets so
much harder.

00:26:30.970 --> 00:26:34.120
And I think that's when we started
layering in executives who had

00:26:34.120 --> 00:26:35.080
done it before.

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:38.730
Because now we have the experience of
people who aren't like the founders,

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:40.440
aren't learning on the job,
they've done it before,

00:26:40.440 --> 00:26:43.020
they've done it three times
before very successfully.

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:46.220
And it just is much easier to
run the business that way.

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:48.690
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a good insight of when,

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:51.590
we all want to hire the folks that
have done it before early, right?

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:56.370
Not all of us, but we get attracted
to the box on the resume, but

00:26:56.370 --> 00:26:59.230
maybe that Dunbar number is the right
time to think about it, right?

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:03.792
When did you benefit from folks that maybe
weren't as fluent in the product, but

00:27:03.792 --> 00:27:05.548
were fluent in the processes?

00:27:05.548 --> 00:27:08.302
&gt;&gt; Yeah, if I could do it over,
I'd probably bring them on earlier,

00:27:08.302 --> 00:27:11.746
but at that point, it's just like you're
trying to recruit someone who's very,

00:27:11.746 --> 00:27:15.162
very good at their jobs who are getting
paid tones of money where ever they're at.

00:27:15.162 --> 00:27:19.371
And it's hard to recruit them to your,
you know, little **** start up,

00:27:19.371 --> 00:27:23.511
but you sell your butts off,
actually you never stop selling your butts

00:27:23.511 --> 00:27:26.490
off about your mission,
your vision as a company.

00:27:26.490 --> 00:27:31.035
And so being able to deliver that
message of what you're trying to do and

00:27:31.035 --> 00:27:34.232
also showing the traction,
showing the growth.

00:27:34.232 --> 00:27:37.458
When you see that graph,
everyone wants to be a part of it.

00:27:37.458 --> 00:27:38.502
&gt;&gt; They do, yeah.

00:27:38.502 --> 00:27:40.600
Was there a moment,
recruiting was always hard, but

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:42.100
was there a moment when it got easier?

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:43.770
&gt;&gt; Practice.

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:47.320
Practicing gets easier so I'd say maybe-
&gt;&gt; But from a brand perspective.

00:27:47.320 --> 00:27:48.240
Get to the point?

00:27:49.790 --> 00:27:54.920
Did Sequoia or others, or Press or
you, I want to ask a related question,

00:27:54.920 --> 00:27:57.010
but did it get,
in some ways easier when you became

00:27:58.110 --> 00:28:01.960
a more interesting startup
to a broader community?

00:28:01.960 --> 00:28:06.890
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so we're a YC company,
we're also in the Sequoia portfolio.

00:28:06.890 --> 00:28:09.410
We flash that to our advantage.

00:28:09.410 --> 00:28:14.270
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
[LAUGH] So good.

00:28:14.270 --> 00:28:18.320
So Harry,
any learnings on hiring mistakes or

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:20.440
great hire you made,
that moved the needle for you?

00:28:22.030 --> 00:28:23.450
&gt;&gt; Yeah so I can actually talk to one.

00:28:23.450 --> 00:28:28.960
You mentioned earlier just like realizing
that, you need to hire good managers.

00:28:28.960 --> 00:28:31.320
It was a big moment for
us in the past couple of years and

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:33.270
that was actually one of
the early mistakes we made,

00:28:33.270 --> 00:28:37.130
which is one of our best IC's
thought he wanted to be a manager.

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:40.430
And we really wanted him
to managed a team too.

00:28:40.430 --> 00:28:43.020
He hadn't actually had
experience doing that before and

00:28:43.020 --> 00:28:45.330
when we put him in that role,
he struggled.

00:28:45.330 --> 00:28:49.930
And that had some, that put us
through a tough six months, right?

00:28:49.930 --> 00:28:53.780
And I think we learned a lesson early on,
it's like you want people who have already

00:28:53.780 --> 00:28:57.330
done it once before because even though
you may trust somebody and they're awesome

00:28:57.330 --> 00:29:02.120
at what they do, it's hard to discount
the value of experience, right?

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:04.190
And that's something,
like just having seen it before,

00:29:04.190 --> 00:29:07.250
the type of problems you can see,
that's made a big difference.

00:29:07.250 --> 00:29:10.530
So for us, like I said,
our first most huge hires,

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:13.380
like, we had somebody in our head
engineering role that didn't pan out and

00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:16.930
our second head of engineering
was just incredible, right?

00:29:16.930 --> 00:29:18.560
He literally made problems disappear.

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:20.830
Like things that we would sit there and
worry about.

00:29:20.830 --> 00:29:22.850
I'd be there and
my co-founder talking about this and

00:29:22.850 --> 00:29:25.580
this people issue and next thing we knew.

00:29:25.580 --> 00:29:28.390
Literally three weeks after we started,
we just never talked about it,

00:29:28.390 --> 00:29:29.290
heard about it again.

00:29:29.290 --> 00:29:31.890
Everyone seemed to be happy and
working harder and staying longer.

00:29:31.890 --> 00:29:34.020
And we didn't really feel like we
were doing anything different.

00:29:34.020 --> 00:29:35.450
We just had somebody
different on the role.

00:29:35.450 --> 00:29:38.440
And-
&gt;&gt; How quickly did you know, 30 days?

00:29:38.440 --> 00:29:39.752
&gt;&gt; What's that?
&gt;&gt; How quickly did you know it

00:29:39.752 --> 00:29:40.869
was impactful, an hour?

00:29:40.869 --> 00:29:43.546
&gt;&gt; Probably it was, so we had
the advantage of already having someone on

00:29:43.546 --> 00:29:46.620
the role, so we sort of knew what
good looked like and what great was.

00:29:46.620 --> 00:29:49.140
And obviously we are always
looking at that,

00:29:49.140 --> 00:29:53.280
but the first three days I think
the difference they are having as a team.

00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:56.649
Even within the first two
weeks a lot of learning, and

00:29:56.649 --> 00:29:59.980
you can just tell from the quality of
question that they're asking, right?

00:29:59.980 --> 00:30:03.140
What are they thinking how
far they planning ahead.

00:30:03.140 --> 00:30:04.042
And then really,

00:30:04.042 --> 00:30:07.330
we started to see a lot of changes happens
in second week in terms of, what I taking

00:30:07.330 --> 00:30:10.750
care I learned throughout the interview
process, get to know the company.

00:30:10.750 --> 00:30:12.970
And also adapting it to there own style,
right.

00:30:12.970 --> 00:30:17.330
And I think, first of for most it's like,
not just hiring for experience but

00:30:17.330 --> 00:30:20.470
hiring for culture fit is really,
really important.

00:30:21.560 --> 00:30:23.921
We have had great hires in the past,

00:30:23.921 --> 00:30:28.977
awesome IC's that we let fester a little
too long, because of cultural things.

00:30:28.977 --> 00:30:33.989
And having to rewind that back a few years
later it was definitely challenging.

00:30:33.989 --> 00:30:35.951
So I think culture fit now,

00:30:35.951 --> 00:30:40.140
like we spend a ton of time
through our interview process.

00:30:40.140 --> 00:30:43.621
We've updated our values,
worked into the interview process,

00:30:43.621 --> 00:30:47.561
each person is responsible for
asking one particular component of that.

00:30:47.561 --> 00:30:50.191
And that's like one of the first
things we evaluate, regardless or

00:30:50.191 --> 00:30:51.758
not whether the person can do their job,

00:30:51.758 --> 00:30:54.713
it's like is this someone we'd work with,
and is this someone who's going

00:30:54.713 --> 00:30:57.507
to fit well really bring their own
individual skill set to the company.

00:30:57.507 --> 00:30:58.515
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a good point.

00:30:58.515 --> 00:31:02.443
Tracy, let me go back to a point you made
about the journey that your customers

00:31:02.443 --> 00:31:06.311
are signing up for 6 and 12 months,
and maybe they're even signing up for

00:31:06.311 --> 00:31:07.960
60 months and 100 months.

00:31:07.960 --> 00:31:11.810
Especially in the early days,
products are super feature poor, right?

00:31:11.810 --> 00:31:16.660
I mean, you had 29 folks sign up
immediately, but it's still, how do you

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:21.450
convince them to go on that journey when
you have incumbents that are feature rich?

00:31:21.450 --> 00:31:21.970
What did you learn?

00:31:23.975 --> 00:31:26.870
&gt;&gt; There's nothing like
adoption in the field.

00:31:26.870 --> 00:31:31.390
So there's certainly lots of incumbents,
some that you know very well.

00:31:31.390 --> 00:31:33.920
There's also a lot of start ups here, and

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:37.610
that are just, if you have any success
at all, your software will be copied.

00:31:37.610 --> 00:31:40.050
You guys are dealing with that right now.

00:31:40.050 --> 00:31:44.180
And so just making sure that we
are developing products that are actually

00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:47.920
being used in the field
has been key to success.

00:31:47.920 --> 00:31:53.765
That regardless of what VP of operations
or CIO's want to deploy, the software

00:31:53.765 --> 00:31:56.275
that's being used on construction site
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:31:56.275 --> 00:32:01.895
&gt;&gt; Like for real is our software, because
we made sure we were building software for

00:32:01.895 --> 00:32:05.805
the superintendents and foremen, and
carpenter, and electricians in a way that

00:32:05.805 --> 00:32:10.782
is actually powerful to them that
actually helps them Do their jobs better.

00:32:10.782 --> 00:32:14.784
&gt;&gt; So this is learning from me, but
a lot of your history is coming in from

00:32:14.784 --> 00:32:17.970
the field, even if the CIO's office or
whoever it is.

00:32:17.970 --> 00:32:21.691
&gt;&gt; We build software for them,
too, so we've got admin panels.

00:32:21.691 --> 00:32:23.019
We have SSO coming out for them.

00:32:23.019 --> 00:32:23.731
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:32:23.731 --> 00:32:27.877
&gt;&gt; We've got beautiful graphs and stuff,
because they are a decision maker and

00:32:27.877 --> 00:32:29.290
that's important to us.

00:32:29.290 --> 00:32:30.990
But I think early days,

00:32:30.990 --> 00:32:33.570
I mean first four years of Plangrid-
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:32:33.570 --> 00:32:38.503
&gt;&gt; We were definitely not prioritizing
products for the people in the office.

00:32:38.503 --> 00:32:40.123
&gt;&gt; And how and
let's just talk about that for

00:32:40.123 --> 00:32:41.890
me because that's always interesting,
right?

00:32:41.890 --> 00:32:44.600
And so, you get in through the field,
they love your product.

00:32:44.600 --> 00:32:49.407
They can adopt it immediately instead of
taking six months, and how did you deal

00:32:49.407 --> 00:32:54.232
with that conflict if a different
vendor had already been selected by.

00:32:54.232 --> 00:32:56.195
Or, any learnings on how to-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:32:56.195 --> 00:32:56.824
&gt;&gt; To deal with that [INAUDIBLE]?

00:32:56.824 --> 00:33:01.342
&gt;&gt; Our industry is super interesting
because we essentially sold a construction

00:33:01.342 --> 00:33:02.030
projects.

00:33:02.030 --> 00:33:05.520
There certain construction companies that
manage like several projects at the time.

00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:08.870
But the beauty of our industry, and
it's also both like the best and

00:33:08.870 --> 00:33:12.990
the worst thing is,
when we're in a construction project,

00:33:12.990 --> 00:33:17.370
there's actually 50 different
customers for us to sell to.

00:33:17.370 --> 00:33:19.250
There are the carpenters,
there's electricians,

00:33:19.250 --> 00:33:24.640
there's the concrete folks,
the pool installers, the paint person.

00:33:24.640 --> 00:33:28.970
And so, there is this natural virally that
happens within construction projects.

00:33:28.970 --> 00:33:32.170
The foreman starts using Plangrid,
and it's a cultural thing too.

00:33:32.170 --> 00:33:36.110
As a construction person, you just
want to show off your shiny new tool.

00:33:36.110 --> 00:33:36.760
And Plangrid's so

00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:40.680
cool that you sort of want to be the first
one to show everyone else on the project.

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:46.530
And so, we were always laser focused at
building something that was easy to adopt,

00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:47.510
easy to use,

00:33:47.510 --> 00:33:51.860
and that we sort of sparked that network
effect within construction projects.

00:33:51.860 --> 00:33:56.530
So, here's corporate standardizing on
a software, then you've got the field,

00:33:57.820 --> 00:34:00.690
which the person paying for
it really is the building owner.

00:34:00.690 --> 00:34:03.599
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And so, we've just been laser focused

00:34:03.599 --> 00:34:08.428
at getting our software adopted,
At the ground level,

00:34:08.428 --> 00:34:11.426
which is very different than any
other software in the space.

00:34:11.426 --> 00:34:16.023
&gt;&gt; And you spent a lot of time on those
other users that actually don't pay for

00:34:16.023 --> 00:34:17.190
a Plangrid?

00:34:17.190 --> 00:34:19.020
All the other folks in the project,
how do you make them happy too?

00:34:20.620 --> 00:34:25.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we made sure to build something
that was flexible enough that could be

00:34:25.040 --> 00:34:28.290
used by anyone in the architectural,
engineering, and construction industry.

00:34:29.970 --> 00:34:32.660
Prioritizing products
is a key factor here.

00:34:32.660 --> 00:34:33.550
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; We didn't

00:34:33.550 --> 00:34:35.990
want to build something that was just for
one profile.

00:34:35.990 --> 00:34:40.480
We wanted it to be flexible enough that it
could be used by everyone in the industry.

00:34:41.510 --> 00:34:42.295
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
So, Solomon,

00:34:42.295 --> 00:34:45.184
let's talk a varying of these sort of
competition, a long journey for you.

00:34:45.184 --> 00:34:51.867
I feel like Dockers a product and
a vendor that people are rooting for.

00:34:51.867 --> 00:34:54.591
I think there are a lot of
the Docker fans, I would say.

00:34:54.591 --> 00:34:58.492
How do you invest in that,
how do you return that social contract in

00:34:58.492 --> 00:35:02.340
the product side especially as
competition increases, right?

00:35:02.340 --> 00:35:05.496
As you prove yourself, everyone wants
to do something in the broad container

00:35:05.496 --> 00:35:07.302
the market, competition's everywhere.

00:35:07.302 --> 00:35:11.296
How do you keep that like
100Mbps the Docker probably has?

00:35:11.296 --> 00:35:15.260
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so,
&gt;&gt; Do you measure MBps?

00:35:15.260 --> 00:35:17.440
&gt;&gt; We measure it well,
[LAUGH] we measure in Mbps.

00:35:17.440 --> 00:35:18.438
&gt;&gt; Happiness?

00:35:18.438 --> 00:35:19.770
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes, we do.

00:35:19.770 --> 00:35:21.321
The problem for us is, who do we ask,

00:35:21.321 --> 00:35:23.387
because there's a lot
of different audiences.

00:35:23.387 --> 00:35:25.953
Actually, it's a really
interesting comparison to,

00:35:25.953 --> 00:35:28.637
I think the same thing is
happening in a lot of industries.

00:35:28.637 --> 00:35:31.110
There was a sort of a bottom-up
go to market, right?

00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:35.998
Where, first you get
the partitioners on boards, and

00:35:35.998 --> 00:35:40.682
then, you get their bosses on board,
maybe later.

00:35:40.682 --> 00:35:45.524
Maybe they learn about your
product only once, Dozens or

00:35:45.524 --> 00:35:47.900
hundreds of people in
the organization are using it.

00:35:47.900 --> 00:35:49.230
&gt;&gt; Even today at Docker?

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:52.730
&gt;&gt; So it's, yeah, so we use that-
&gt;&gt; There's a real gap in some-

00:35:52.730 --> 00:35:54.499
&gt;&gt; We use that heavily, yes, yeah.

00:35:54.499 --> 00:35:58.409
And so today, as a business,
we target enterprises,

00:35:58.409 --> 00:36:01.560
very large businesses, that is our focus.

00:36:01.560 --> 00:36:04.882
We sell also to small businesses,
we're kind of in a similar situation,

00:36:04.882 --> 00:36:06.896
you've got the tens of thousands and then,

00:36:06.896 --> 00:36:09.357
the majority of the revenue
comes from a few hundred.

00:36:09.357 --> 00:36:13.869
But all of them are in this
relationship with us as a vendor,

00:36:13.869 --> 00:36:18.380
because someone on their team
at some point said, new app,

00:36:18.380 --> 00:36:22.510
awesome Docker,
I went to meetup, sounds great.

00:36:22.510 --> 00:36:24.870
Or, I saw a discussion online about it.

00:36:24.870 --> 00:36:29.290
So, we owe everything to that community,
and

00:36:29.290 --> 00:36:31.290
there's a lot of positive
vibes in that community.

00:36:31.290 --> 00:36:34.390
Honestly, it's pretty insane, if you've
ever been to a Docker meetup or DockerCon.

00:36:34.390 --> 00:36:36.165
&gt;&gt; DockerCon,
that was like 5,000 people, right?

00:36:36.165 --> 00:36:38.520
&gt;&gt; It's just, it's ridiculous.

00:36:38.520 --> 00:36:40.368
&gt;&gt; So, in the early days,
how do you facilitate that?

00:36:40.368 --> 00:36:41.240
What are the learnings?

00:36:41.240 --> 00:36:43.440
How do you get that flywheel going?

00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:46.660
&gt;&gt; Honestly, we started something
very small and the day, ten people,

00:36:46.660 --> 00:36:50.560
we showed it to a few people one by one.

00:36:50.560 --> 00:36:55.740
And then, ten of them came back to our
office saying, hey, we want to know more.

00:36:55.740 --> 00:36:58.880
And it was just the most
fun three hours of my life.

00:36:58.880 --> 00:37:01.720
Basically we were just talking about all
the cool stuff that we could build with

00:37:01.720 --> 00:37:04.560
Docker and then they came back the week
after that, and it was 40 people, and

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:05.312
then it was 100.

00:37:05.312 --> 00:37:09.790
So pretty, there was a first week
of the existence of Docker, right?

00:37:09.790 --> 00:37:11.564
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And so, right away we saw,

00:37:11.564 --> 00:37:15.951
okay, there's something special going on
here, and there is a community forming and

00:37:15.951 --> 00:37:19.496
they're basically saying, hey you,
the company who started this,

00:37:19.496 --> 00:37:20.848
you're the janitor now.

00:37:20.848 --> 00:37:25.597
Like make sure that organize things for
us, because this is our thing now.

00:37:25.597 --> 00:37:30.245
And so, we just took that job
really seriously of enabling that

00:37:30.245 --> 00:37:33.410
community to do whatever it decides to do.

00:37:33.410 --> 00:37:35.900
So, we've been following
that community ever since.

00:37:35.900 --> 00:37:38.652
And just removing obstacles for
that community to grow,

00:37:38.652 --> 00:37:39.922
do more things together.

00:37:39.922 --> 00:37:43.110
And so we have a whole team that's focused
on that, just enabling the community.

00:37:43.110 --> 00:37:44.520
We have all these cool programs.

00:37:44.520 --> 00:37:46.715
We have, I think,
120 meetups in the world.

00:37:46.715 --> 00:37:50.510
100,000 people have-
&gt;&gt; 120 meetups a year, all right.

00:37:50.510 --> 00:37:53.523
No, so it's 120 locations in the world,

00:37:53.523 --> 00:37:57.346
each with an average of a meet-up
every two months or so.

00:37:57.346 --> 00:37:58.002
&gt;&gt; Got it.

00:37:58.002 --> 00:38:00.123
&gt;&gt; And then 100, 000 people I think,

00:38:00.123 --> 00:38:03.342
overall have showed up at least
once to one of these meet-ups.

00:38:03.342 --> 00:38:09.880
There was a captains program which
is sort of like elites power user,

00:38:09.880 --> 00:38:14.964
where the most savvy and
articulate users of Docker.

00:38:14.964 --> 00:38:19.733
We kind of promote them, and pay them for
them to go and speak at events,

00:38:19.733 --> 00:38:23.168
and they just become these
stars in the community.

00:38:23.168 --> 00:38:26.820
It's a huge investment for us, but
all of it is about giving back.

00:38:26.820 --> 00:38:27.830
&gt;&gt; Got it.
How many captains

00:38:27.830 --> 00:38:28.600
are there out of curiosity?

00:38:28.600 --> 00:38:30.878
&gt;&gt; I think now, there's maybe 20, 30?

00:38:30.878 --> 00:38:32.220
It's a small number.

00:38:33.380 --> 00:38:34.465
&gt;&gt; It sounds like a lot to some of us.

00:38:34.465 --> 00:38:36.090
[LAUGH] Yeah, it's a lot of captains.

00:38:36.090 --> 00:38:37.130
&gt;&gt; It's a lot of captains, yeah.

00:38:37.130 --> 00:38:38.570
&gt;&gt; But you said, you had 400 employees.

00:38:38.570 --> 00:38:39.570
I mean, they're not employees, but

00:38:39.570 --> 00:38:41.990
it's a significant amount of
your extended family, right?

00:38:41.990 --> 00:38:42.938
&gt;&gt; Yes, correct.

00:38:42.938 --> 00:38:47.295
Yeah, and so anyway, when you see
a community, when you see anyone super

00:38:47.295 --> 00:38:51.868
excited about what you're doing,
just drop everything you're doing, and

00:38:51.868 --> 00:38:54.701
make sure you put themselves
at their service.

00:38:54.701 --> 00:38:58.774
In our case, it's the developers,
using an open source tool, but

00:38:58.774 --> 00:39:01.930
it looks like there's
something similar going on.

00:39:01.930 --> 00:39:05.069
I'm sure there's,
I'm going to say it wrong, but

00:39:05.069 --> 00:39:09.631
whatever the job is of someone actually
working on a construction site,

00:39:09.631 --> 00:39:13.254
some of them, I'm sure,
are just huge fans of the tool.

00:39:13.254 --> 00:39:18.210
And they go everywhere, and
they just tell all their buddies about it.

00:39:18.210 --> 00:39:22.348
And, I think that's how
consumer products used to grow.

00:39:22.348 --> 00:39:23.045
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:39:23.045 --> 00:39:25.079
&gt;&gt; I think now, it's increasingly and

00:39:25.079 --> 00:39:28.070
seeing the best B2B products
also grow that way.

00:39:28.070 --> 00:39:29.340
&gt;&gt; So let's make that our last question.

00:39:29.340 --> 00:39:31.270
I want to save a few minutes for
questions so we have it.

00:39:31.270 --> 00:39:34.480
But related to that, I think I
already know the answer for Docker.

00:39:34.480 --> 00:39:36.310
Lets just do Plangrid and Lob.

00:39:36.310 --> 00:39:40.550
But, Tracy today, what percent of your new
customers come from a word of mouth or

00:39:40.550 --> 00:39:41.340
a brand?

00:39:41.340 --> 00:39:46.140
Or, does all these great good will
that you built across the industry,

00:39:46.140 --> 00:39:47.260
how was that pay off today?

00:39:47.260 --> 00:39:50.130
&gt;&gt; I mean we definitely ship
Facebook ads and stuff, but

00:39:50.130 --> 00:39:56.310
most of our new customers comes from
just word of mouth organic growth.

00:39:56.310 --> 00:39:59.400
Which is awesome, but
then it's also uncontrollable, right?

00:39:59.400 --> 00:40:03.830
Just some, it kind of comes naturally to
us, and we don't know what sparks it.

00:40:03.830 --> 00:40:07.460
But, we know that if we make our clients
happy and we build a great product for

00:40:07.460 --> 00:40:09.810
them, that that does continue to happen.

00:40:09.810 --> 00:40:10.578
Year after year.

00:40:10.578 --> 00:40:12.104
&gt;&gt; Yeah, is that happening at Lob yet?

00:40:12.104 --> 00:40:13.947
Do you get most of your
customers from the base?

00:40:13.947 --> 00:40:18.321
&gt;&gt; Yeah, our base customers are the folks
that are referring a good portion of our

00:40:18.321 --> 00:40:19.082
[CROSSTALK].

00:40:19.082 --> 00:40:21.510
&gt;&gt; So you're not having to send all
the emails yourself today anymore, right?

00:40:21.510 --> 00:40:25.134
&gt;&gt; No, I mean, what we've actually found,
similar to what Solomon was saying,

00:40:25.134 --> 00:40:28.651
one of the things that's interesting,
we have these folks have been at three

00:40:28.651 --> 00:40:32.450
different companies in a four-year span,
and have used Lob at three companies.

00:40:32.450 --> 00:40:33.657
&gt;&gt; That's when you know it's working.

00:40:33.657 --> 00:40:35.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we see this all the time.

00:40:35.440 --> 00:40:38.960
And it's great because every time I get an
email from them, it's a different company.

00:40:38.960 --> 00:40:39.800
Hey, I want to bring you here.

00:40:39.800 --> 00:40:41.980
I was like great,
I love the challenge from you, right?

00:40:41.980 --> 00:40:45.167
And you want those folks who
are going to be your evangelists,

00:40:45.167 --> 00:40:49.307
who love your product, everywhere they go,
they going to find a way to use you,

00:40:49.307 --> 00:40:50.980
because they like it, right?

00:40:50.980 --> 00:40:53.785
And that's really important,
and that played a big part for

00:40:53.785 --> 00:40:57.600
some of our regular customers going to
different companies, bringing us there.

00:40:57.600 --> 00:40:59.172
And then you also get the referrals,

00:40:59.172 --> 00:41:01.870
especially what you want to do is
you want to create word of mouth.

00:41:01.870 --> 00:41:04.950
And we've found this to be very
successful at conferences, right?

00:41:04.950 --> 00:41:08.370
Where we already have a good
foundational layer of companies.

00:41:08.370 --> 00:41:11.600
So for us, Fintech and
the lending space is a big one, right?

00:41:11.600 --> 00:41:13.730
So we're going out, we're at LendIt,

00:41:13.730 --> 00:41:16.375
and our customers will
bring their friends over.

00:41:16.375 --> 00:41:18.510
For our booth, well yeah,
these guys are great, right?

00:41:18.510 --> 00:41:21.664
And there's nothing like a positive
referral for all from someone who is

00:41:21.664 --> 00:41:24.990
existing customer to somebody else
that's doing something very similar.

00:41:24.990 --> 00:41:28.060
And that is part of the reason
why we go every single year.

00:41:28.060 --> 00:41:30.930
And we sit there, we smile, we host,
customer appreciative of that.

00:41:30.930 --> 00:41:34.570
And eventually we'd love to get to
the point where we can do like a Lob Con,

00:41:34.570 --> 00:41:36.120
get a bunch of folks together.

00:41:36.120 --> 00:41:37.430
&gt;&gt; You may be ready already, actually.

00:41:37.430 --> 00:41:38.504
I think, right?

00:41:38.504 --> 00:41:39.357
You may be ready.

00:41:39.357 --> 00:41:40.849
All right.
We'll keep going on this, but

00:41:40.849 --> 00:41:41.865
let's take some questions.

00:41:41.865 --> 00:41:45.018
Anyone got any great questions for
the panel?

00:41:45.018 --> 00:41:49.972
&gt;&gt; I was thinking what was one past
experience that you think helped

00:41:49.972 --> 00:41:53.560
you the most while
starting your own company?

00:41:54.950 --> 00:41:56.100
&gt;&gt; Jason, can you repeat the question?

00:41:56.100 --> 00:41:57.735
&gt;&gt; I can.

00:41:57.735 --> 00:41:59.884
&gt;&gt; The question was what was one- You're,
sure right.

00:41:59.884 --> 00:42:01.500
I like redundancy.

00:42:01.500 --> 00:42:02.813
I worry.

00:42:02.813 --> 00:42:05.383
I like five nines of uptime.

00:42:05.383 --> 00:42:08.647
What was one experience prior to starting
your company that actually helped

00:42:08.647 --> 00:42:09.769
you get it off the ground?

00:42:09.769 --> 00:42:14.142
&gt;&gt; So two of the five co-founders of
Plangrid had built buildings before, and

00:42:14.142 --> 00:42:16.550
the other three were building software.

00:42:17.910 --> 00:42:23.536
Just our ability to rally resources and
people, and build something together,

00:42:23.536 --> 00:42:28.680
the ability to work together really
helped us work in a startup together.

00:42:28.680 --> 00:42:30.480
It just feels like
the medium's changed for me.

00:42:30.480 --> 00:42:37.910
Instead of concrete and brick,
it's people in code and more people.

00:42:37.910 --> 00:42:40.360
&gt;&gt; Do you think you can penetrate
the industry with no domain expertise?

00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:43.680
I mean, I am sure it's been done, but
what's your learning on domain expertise?

00:42:44.990 --> 00:42:46.970
&gt;&gt; I think it's possible.

00:42:46.970 --> 00:42:50.510
You just have to become an expert fast,
so somehow you have to compensate for it.

00:42:51.940 --> 00:42:53.340
&gt;&gt; I can comment on that one a little bit.

00:42:53.340 --> 00:42:55.455
I didn't have any domain
expertise in mail,

00:42:55.455 --> 00:42:58.246
I don't think a ton of people
had domain expertise in mail.

00:42:58.246 --> 00:43:00.775
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I am an expert now, though, and

00:43:00.775 --> 00:43:03.120
certainly that's something I've learned.

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:05.810
But for me past experience, right?

00:43:05.810 --> 00:43:07.920
I came out of college I went to Microsoft.

00:43:07.920 --> 00:43:11.015
And this is like the last place in
the world you'll expect to send mail and

00:43:11.015 --> 00:43:12.440
have it be effective.

00:43:12.440 --> 00:43:17.388
One of the projects I worked on there, we
follow multiple different privacy reasons,

00:43:17.388 --> 00:43:19.365
like we're not allowed to email.

00:43:19.365 --> 00:43:23.497
We couldn't do it through the channel for
another couple of reasons, and my only

00:43:23.497 --> 00:43:27.874
option was either calling everybody which
didn't seem very feasible at the time, or

00:43:27.874 --> 00:43:30.480
sending people these nice little kits.

00:43:30.480 --> 00:43:33.767
And it turns out like the best performing
thing was mail, and I ended up

00:43:33.767 --> 00:43:37.625
spending three months in hundreds of
thousands of dollars, it's a huge budget.

00:43:37.625 --> 00:43:41.899
So working on this, and realizing that
this is something like people actually do,

00:43:41.899 --> 00:43:45.120
and companies like Microsoft
do everyday and it works.

00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:47.700
But it's really hard and it's ancient.

00:43:47.700 --> 00:43:51.851
We had people suggesting us,
like automation, paying tens of thousand

00:43:51.851 --> 00:43:56.640
dollars for implementation to get an FTP
folder that we drop an Excel file into.

00:43:56.640 --> 00:43:59.849
And it seems sort of ridiculous but
I was part of the inspiration for

00:43:59.849 --> 00:44:01.350
how we started working in Lob.

00:44:03.562 --> 00:44:04.540
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:44:04.540 --> 00:44:08.287
&gt;&gt; What are your thoughts about big
companies who can offer a competing

00:44:08.287 --> 00:44:12.570
service or product that can damage
their operations expertise like Amazon?

00:44:13.886 --> 00:44:18.500
&gt;&gt; Yeah, or let's how do you deal with
big companies that can do it all, right?

00:44:18.500 --> 00:44:19.393
How do you deal with that situation?

00:44:19.393 --> 00:44:20.208
&gt;&gt; Sure, yes.

00:44:20.208 --> 00:44:23.013
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think there's probably,

00:44:23.013 --> 00:44:26.760
there's a generic answer that's true for
every business, and then there's for

00:44:26.760 --> 00:44:30.050
every startup, and then there is
maybe some domain specific answers.

00:44:30.050 --> 00:44:33.659
I think, personally I think
something that's true for

00:44:33.659 --> 00:44:37.210
all startups is that you have focus and
you have speed.

00:44:37.210 --> 00:44:41.015
And I had never worked at
a large company before, so

00:44:41.015 --> 00:44:47.240
I did not appreciate exactly the advantage
we have as a smaller company.

00:44:47.240 --> 00:44:49.701
But now that we're growing a little bit,

00:44:49.701 --> 00:44:52.455
I can already tell what
happens when you grow.

00:44:52.455 --> 00:44:53.848
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so

00:44:53.848 --> 00:44:58.048
the goal really is to take advantage
of our small size while we're small,

00:44:58.048 --> 00:45:00.770
faster than we accumulate
problems as we grow.

00:45:00.770 --> 00:45:05.628
When you're big and you have a successful
business, everyone working there,

00:45:05.628 --> 00:45:08.230
the majority of people working there.

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:12.205
First of all we're not there at the time
when the business became successful, so

00:45:12.205 --> 00:45:16.140
they all, it's long forgotten how they
became successful in the first place.

00:45:16.140 --> 00:45:20.040
They're administrating
an existing cash cow.

00:45:20.040 --> 00:45:23.720
And so there's a completely kind of
people, generally speaking, right?

00:45:23.720 --> 00:45:24.910
Except maybe the very top.

00:45:24.910 --> 00:45:27.792
You got a few VPs there,
the original guy who did it and

00:45:27.792 --> 00:45:29.534
then he's running something.

00:45:29.534 --> 00:45:33.150
But usually these
are people who at a large,

00:45:33.150 --> 00:45:38.235
your average large company,
they're not united as one group

00:45:38.235 --> 00:45:43.765
to make that company succeed
against outside threats like you.

00:45:43.765 --> 00:45:48.690
They are focused usually on their career,
are they going to get promoted?

00:45:48.690 --> 00:45:50.380
Are they going to get
more people under them?

00:45:52.210 --> 00:45:55.240
It's inside facing rather
than outside facing.

00:45:55.240 --> 00:45:57.770
And most large companies are like that.

00:45:57.770 --> 00:46:02.255
And that's an advantage,
because they're not looking for threats.

00:46:02.255 --> 00:46:04.370
The other advantage is focus.

00:46:04.370 --> 00:46:06.700
Large companies tend to
be doing a lot of things.

00:46:06.700 --> 00:46:11.087
And when you're competing with a company
that has 10,000 people and you have ten,

00:46:11.087 --> 00:46:12.680
it might seem really daunting.

00:46:12.680 --> 00:46:17.633
But the particular parts of that company
that is actually competing with you, for

00:46:17.633 --> 00:46:19.950
all you know might also be ten people.

00:46:19.950 --> 00:46:23.416
And they might be underfunded, they might
be on the wrong side of the big boss who

00:46:23.416 --> 00:46:25.620
just doesn't care about their product.

00:46:25.620 --> 00:46:29.717
And over time, as you start competing,
you'll find out which ones are serious

00:46:29.717 --> 00:46:32.420
threats and
which ones are basically to be ignored.

00:46:32.420 --> 00:46:33.870
&gt;&gt; It's hard to tell from the outside.

00:46:33.870 --> 00:46:38.423
&gt;&gt; Hard to tell but as you start becoming
an expert, and you talk to customers,

00:46:38.423 --> 00:46:40.000
I mean it's pretty easy.

00:46:40.000 --> 00:46:43.324
Customers are going to say well I'm
thinking about you and these other two,

00:46:43.324 --> 00:46:44.090
three things.

00:46:44.090 --> 00:46:47.955
And some names are going to come up a lot,
and some of them will be other startups,

00:46:47.955 --> 00:46:51.840
some of them will be other big companies,
and then you narrow down your focus.

00:46:51.840 --> 00:46:56.098
And you figure out okay, what makes that
big company smarter than the others?

00:46:56.098 --> 00:46:59.920
But you can break down the problem,
basically.

00:47:01.910 --> 00:47:02.490
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:47:02.490 --> 00:47:05.983
&gt;&gt; What do you think the biggest factors
that lead customers to tell other

00:47:05.983 --> 00:47:07.590
customers about your product?

00:47:07.590 --> 00:47:09.053
&gt;&gt; That's the magic question,

00:47:09.053 --> 00:47:12.630
what do you think the biggest factors
are that create this word of mouth?

00:47:12.630 --> 00:47:15.130
They get other customers to refer you out.

00:47:15.130 --> 00:47:16.667
What can you point to specifically?

00:47:16.667 --> 00:47:23.240
&gt;&gt; I mean the short answer is good
product, something that saves them time.

00:47:23.240 --> 00:47:25.598
At the end of the day
that's all we care about,

00:47:25.598 --> 00:47:29.000
we've got this one precious
thing that we don't get more of.

00:47:29.000 --> 00:47:32.651
And because PlanGrid absolutely
saves our users time,

00:47:32.651 --> 00:47:35.300
it's why they've told everyone else.

00:47:35.300 --> 00:47:37.320
&gt;&gt; But
let me just buried in that question.

00:47:37.320 --> 00:47:40.840
So you had the great story of
the customer that left, the three X,

00:47:40.840 --> 00:47:42.470
that's a great story.

00:47:42.470 --> 00:47:45.690
But in B2B it's hard for
that to happen in three weeks, right?

00:47:45.690 --> 00:47:47.182
How long did that take to get that,

00:47:47.182 --> 00:47:49.920
there's three customers out of
that first grade experience?

00:47:49.920 --> 00:47:53.287
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think the first thing I
realized is customers aren't going to just

00:47:53.287 --> 00:47:55.340
all of a sudden start
referring them to you.

00:47:55.340 --> 00:47:57.750
They want to build a relationship and
trust you.

00:47:57.750 --> 00:48:01.628
And the only reason why that happened is
because the first year It went from this

00:48:01.628 --> 00:48:04.801
particular individual,
like start off as a very, very small,

00:48:04.801 --> 00:48:08.268
like couple of $1,000 a month customer and
grew to be like 50,

00:48:08.268 --> 00:48:12.890
$60,000 a month customer, very,
very large in a short period of time.

00:48:12.890 --> 00:48:16.332
And we did a lot, like in a lot of things,
we are very transparent,

00:48:16.332 --> 00:48:20.350
like hey, we can't get this done in
this time frame, here's what we can do.

00:48:20.350 --> 00:48:24.690
I had a cellphone number we
frequently text when things go wrong.

00:48:24.690 --> 00:48:27.238
And for us, I think the biggest
reason why he actually went and

00:48:27.238 --> 00:48:30.472
referred us is because of how we handled
things when things didn't go the way we

00:48:30.472 --> 00:48:31.260
planned, right?

00:48:31.260 --> 00:48:35.610
Everybody wants projects to go perfectly,
launch on time.

00:48:35.610 --> 00:48:38.270
And the reality is it doesn't
always happen that way.

00:48:38.270 --> 00:48:41.901
And sometimes there's factors out of your
control, but I think the way that we

00:48:41.901 --> 00:48:45.600
handle ourselves when things didn't go
planned was a huge vote in confidence.

00:48:45.600 --> 00:48:49.380
That this is a company that we can trust
because we were watching people scramble.

00:48:49.380 --> 00:48:51.507
And I remember there is once instance,

00:48:51.507 --> 00:48:55.870
around Christmas some deliveries
didn't happen for key customers.

00:48:55.870 --> 00:48:58.700
We went out and drove out, and
just delivered it, because I was yeah,

00:48:58.700 --> 00:49:01.960
the guy's down the street is not a big
deal, we'll just go and get it done.

00:49:01.960 --> 00:49:03.060
And he was hugely appreciative.

00:49:03.060 --> 00:49:04.630
I know it was a small thing, right?

00:49:04.630 --> 00:49:07.112
And it was a very small
campaign that he was doing.

00:49:07.112 --> 00:49:11.534
But stories like that that he clings on
to, when he goes to a new company, and so

00:49:11.534 --> 00:49:15.690
the reason I worked with these guys is
because they did all this for us, right?

00:49:15.690 --> 00:49:19.650
And when things were not going well, they
still stepped up and did the right thing.

00:49:19.650 --> 00:49:21.330
And that's why they love us today.

00:49:21.330 --> 00:49:23.085
But I think it starts with
having your product, right?

00:49:23.085 --> 00:49:24.760
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; If your product's not good,

00:49:24.760 --> 00:49:27.900
no matter what you do for the customer,
he's probably not going to take you.

00:49:27.900 --> 00:49:30.414
Because then he is
putting his job at risk.

00:49:30.414 --> 00:49:31.227
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:49:31.227 --> 00:49:31.836
&gt;&gt; You should wrap up right there.

00:49:31.836 --> 00:49:32.747
&gt;&gt; Wrap up right there?

00:49:32.747 --> 00:49:33.803
